Tier 5 -- adapter conformance, shape B (reaper guest, real CBSD).

One conformance suite run against every adapter, asserting identical
contracts: same fields, same error shapes, same exit-code discipline. The real
side drives an actual jail through its lifecycle. This is the tier that breaks
first when CBSD changes an invocation convention. See TESTING.md §6.

THE SUITE ITSELF is tests/conformance/:

  tests/conformance/vectors.tsv   function, args, stdout shape, exit status
  tests/conformance/run.subr      the runner: three assertions per vector --
                                  the status, the shape of every line, and
                                  that a function which promised output
                                  produced some

and it is run against three adapters, in three places:

  tests/tier4/t_conformance_mock.sh    the mock, on the workstation
  tests/tier5/t_conformance_pseudo.sh  the pseudo-cluster adapter, in the guest
  tests/tier5/t_conformance_real.sh    lib/adapter.subr, against real CBSD

The world the vectors assume is named with placeholders rather than fixture
names, and each file declares its own:

  SEANCE_CONF_JAIL     a jail-type guest that exists          web01
  SEANCE_CONF_VM       a bhyve guest that exists              db01
  SEANCE_CONF_HELD     a guest in slave mode                  arc01
  SEANCE_CONF_ABSENT   a name no guest has              nosuchguest
  SEANCE_CONF_BADNAME  a name no guest may have               UPPER

THE FILES

  t_conformance_pseudo.sh   the vectors against tests/cluster/adapter-pseudo.subr.
                            No cluster: the pseudo adapter's guests are ZFS
                            datasets, so all it needs is a pool, which is why
                            this runs in the guest and not on the workstation.
  t_conformance_real.sh     the vectors against lib/adapter.subr on a real
                            CBSD node, plus the layout claims the shared
                            vectors cannot carry (which path a fact is, where
                            CBSD put the datasets, which way the sysdir
                            symlink points).
  t_lifecycle_real.sh       one real jail through start, hold, refuse,
                            release, stop, unregister, register, start -- and
                            the dataset resolution against a real replica.
  t_promote_real.sh         one `seance repl` tick on a real node, then the
                            successor's half of a promotion: a real jail
                            registers and starts out of the configuration
                            mirror and nothing else.

Each of the three real files builds and destroys its own CBSD node
(tests/shapeb/lib/shapeb.subr, about 19 seconds), for the reason tier 6's
stages each build their own cluster: a file that inherited a world another file
left behind would be asserting against a world nobody described.

THE SUBSTRATE, and the one thing that must not be got wrong: `cbsd initenv`
starts cbsdd and cbsdrsyncd, both of which hold files open under the reset
dataset, and `reaper reset` refuses to roll a dataset back while anything has
files open under it. shapeb_up therefore arms shapeb_down through t_at_exit
BEFORE it creates anything, and t_lifecycle_real.sh and t_promote_real.sh end
with shapeb_down_and_check, which tears the node down and then asserts that no
dataset, no mount and no process is left. Tier 6 runs next, in the same
session, on the same dataset.

WHAT THIS TIER SETTLED (it was written as a list of UNVERIFIED items; they are
now assertions, and docs/cbsd-module-notes.md §6, §8 and §10 carry the
captures):

  * a real jail in slave mode refuses `cbsd jstart` -- D-21's lever, watched
    happening, including that the jail is still not running afterwards;
  * `cbsd jstatus jname=<n> invert=1` for a guest that EXISTS exits 0 and
    prints its jid, non-zero while it runs (D-47);
  * the dataset layout for both guest types on a zfsfeat=1 node, and both
    symlinks a VM gets;
  * adapter_guest_datasets resolves the MOUNTED PATH first, against a replica
    really received under a standby tree and given CBSD's expected mountpoint
    (D-71) -- and falls back rather than resolving to the whole workdir
    dataset when nothing is mounted there;
  * a JAIL's configuration is not inside its dataset and a VM's is (D-77/D-82),
    measured rather than assumed by type;
  * and the mirror: a real jail registers and starts from a
    promote_sys_restore out of a replica of `<pool>/seance-sys` (D-82, D-84);
  * `seance failback-assist <g> unregister` leaves nothing behind in
    ${jailrcconfdir}: junregister writes its own export of the guest as it
    goes and nothing ever removes it, so before the fix one failback made this
    node skip a phantom guest on stderr in every listing for ever. Both halves
    are measured -- CBSD's behaviour raw, then seance's answer to it.

WHAT IT FOUND, which is what the tier is for. Three CBSD defects and three
adapter fixes; every one is written up in docs/cbsd-module-notes.md §8 and
pinned by a tier-4 row so it cannot come back at workstation speed:

  * `cbsd emulator <name>` cannot answer on FreeBSD 15.1 -- SQLite 3.53.3
    rejects its double-quoted literal and CBSD's builtin swallows the parse
    error. adapter_guest_type reads the listing row instead;
  * `cbsd jls jname=<x>` answers with OTHER guests' rows from its unregistered
    area. The adapter filters by name before parsing;
  * CBSD's mutating verbs print on stdout, refusals included. The [no output]
    adapter functions send that to stderr.

STILL UNVERIFIED, and it needs hardware rather than another test: everything in
docs/DRILLS.md -- the fencing drills against a real BMC, and a real two-node
failover. Also a bhyve guest that has ever BOOTED (see notes §10); tier 5
creates one and asserts its layout, which is all seance's own paths read.

Files named t_*.sh are tests; anything else here is ignored.
